WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.535
Data minimization begins with a firm
exclusion policy.

2
00:00:03.615 --> 00:00:07.682
Passwords, password-reset secrets, API
keys, bearer and refresh tokens,

3
00:00:07.762 --> 00:00:11.959
raw session cookies, private keys, signing
secrets, database credentials,

4
00:00:12.039 --> 00:00:16.236
and one-time authentication codes should
not enter application telemetry.

5
00:00:16.316 --> 00:00:21.030
These values provide little investigative
benefit compared with the damage caused by

6
00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:21.820
disclosure.

7
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:26.549
Logging all headers by default is
incompatible with that rule because authorization

8
00:00:26.629 --> 00:00:29.793
and cookie headers commonly carry reusable
credentials.

9
00:00:29.873 --> 00:00:34.134
Select a small allowlist of safe headers
only when a defined use requires them.

10
00:00:34.214 --> 00:00:38.475
Other values require contextual decisions
rather than universal inclusion.

11
00:00:38.555 --> 00:00:42.623
Email addresses, phone numbers, network
addresses, user-agent strings,

12
00:00:42.703 --> 00:00:46.448
query parameters, payment-related data,
message bodies, uploads,

13
00:00:46.528 --> 00:00:49.434
and customer-generated text may aid
investigation,

14
00:00:49.514 --> 00:00:52.871
yet may also be personal, regulated, or
business-sensitive.

15
00:00:52.951 --> 00:00:55.921
Decide by purpose, access, retention,
jurisdiction,

16
00:00:56.001 --> 00:00:58.519
and the telemetry system's protection
level.

17
00:00:58.599 --> 00:01:02.215
User and tenant identifiers can be
essential correlation fields,

18
00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:04.555
but they are not automatically harmless.

19
00:01:04.635 --> 00:01:06.959
Record them only under an explicit policy.

20
00:01:07.039 --> 00:01:09.106
Prevention is stronger than cleanup.

21
00:01:09.186 --> 00:01:13.576
Construct events from an allowlist of
approved fields so unnecessary data never

22
00:01:13.656 --> 00:01:17.336
reaches a serializer, buffer, exporter, or
hosted error reporter.

23
00:01:17.416 --> 00:01:21.936
Redaction remains valuable defense in
depth for framework integrations and known

24
00:01:22.016 --> 00:01:25.567
sensitive keys, but a blacklist can miss
aliases, nested values,

25
00:01:25.647 --> 00:01:28.811
newly added properties, or secrets
embedded in free text.

26
00:01:28.891 --> 00:01:33.153
Inspect the logger's actual serialization
behavior and verify it with tests.

27
00:01:33.233 --> 00:01:36.913
Do not assume an error helper or redaction
option covers every path.

28
00:01:36.993 --> 00:01:38.543
Hashing also requires care.

29
00:01:38.623 --> 00:01:41.141
A stable hash remains linkable across
events,

30
00:01:41.221 --> 00:01:45.805
and identifiers from a small or
predictable input space may be guessed and compared.

31
00:01:45.885 --> 00:01:49.501
Hashing can support a specific
pseudonymous correlation design,

32
00:01:49.581 --> 00:01:54.101
but it is not automatic anonymization and
should not become the default response to

33
00:01:54.181 --> 00:01:55.472
every sensitive field.

34
00:01:55.552 --> 00:01:59.039
Minimize first, then apply redaction,
masking, tokenization,

35
00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:03.380
or pseudonymization only when each
transformation serves a documented need.
